course: everyday
lesson: 402
title: Nightmares after a scary game
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Two nights after Mortal Kombat at Theo's, Leo woke at 3 a.m. absolutely certain a skeleton was under his bed. He lay stiff as a plank for an hour, then sprinted to his parents' room so fast he hit the door frame. In the morning he told everyone he'd «just needed the loo». His little brother Sonny, who misses nothing, asked, «Is it because of the scary game? Can I see the scary game?» «NO,» said Leo, a bit too loudly.
explain: Leo wasn't the only one. Kai slept with the hall light on for a whole week after a game at his older cousin's. Ruby kept seeing one particular scene every time she switched off her lamp. Noah made it worse by watching a scary game clip on his tablet in bed — the last thing in his head before sleep was a monster, so that's what his brain played all night. Zara said loudly she «wasn't scared AT ALL» — and checked behind her bedroom door twice before getting into bed.
explain: Then there was Jayden, who laughed through the scary game while everyone else went pale. Nobody knew if he was really fine or just good at hiding it. And Ollie got a stomach ache every single time Theo said, «Shall we play the scary one again?» That's not a coincidence: worry often shows up in the tummy before you even notice it in your head.
explain: Priya's mum noticed something odd: Priya was drawing monsters in every margin of her homework. She didn't tell her off. She sat down and asked, «Those are good monsters. Where did they come from?» Out it all came — the clip, the dreams, the light. Just saying it out loud made it smaller. They agreed on a plan: no screens in the hour before bed, a funny audiobook instead, and if a bad dream came, she could come and say so with no teasing.
explain: Scary pictures stick because the brain is built to remember danger — it can't easily tell a scene on a screen from a real threat at night. That isn't weakness; it's how brains work. What helps is calm time before bed, talking about it with an adult, and remembering that it was made by artists and actors. And it takes courage to say «that game scared me» — more courage than pretending.
fact: Frightening pictures and scenes often come back in dreams or when the lights go off.
fact: The brain remembers frightening things strongly because noticing danger helped people survive.
fact: Watching something scary just before sleep makes bad dreams more likely.
fact: Many experts advise no screens for about an hour before bed.
fact: Worry and fear can cause a stomach ache or trouble sleeping.
fact: People who say they are not scared at all are sometimes hiding their fear.
fact: Talking about a frightening picture with a trusted adult usually makes it feel smaller.
fact: Monsters in games are made by artists, actors and computers, and are not real.
fact: Younger children are more easily frightened by scary images, which is one reason games have age ratings.
fact: It is normal and not babyish to be scared by a scary game, at any age.
exercise: Why did Leo think a skeleton was under his bed? | A scary game in his mind | He had heard bones rattling under the bed | His brother hid one there for a joke | He had a very high temperature
exercise: Why did Leo say he «just needed the loo»? | He was embarrassed to say he was scared | He really did need the loo, he'd drunk too much | He was sleepwalking | His parents asked him to say it
exercise: Why do scary pictures stick in the brain so well? | The brain remembers danger strongly to keep us safe | The brain gets a bit broken after playing games | Screens burn the pictures into your eyes | Only weak brains remember them
exercise: What made Noah's bad dreams more likely? | Watching a scary clip right before sleep | Sleeping with the bedroom window wide open | Eating an apple at tea | Going to bed too early
exercise: Zara said she wasn't scared but checked behind the door twice. What does this show? | She was more scared than she said | She had lost her slippers in the dark | She was looking for her cat, Biscuit | She is never scared of anything at all
exercise: Why did Kai keep the hall light on for a week? | He was still frightened after the game | The bulb was stuck | His parents forgot to turn it off before bed | He wanted to read his comics all night
exercise: Why did Ollie get a stomach ache when Theo mentioned the game? | Worry can show up as a tummy ache | He had eaten far too many crisps at Theo's | The game made him feel travel sick | He was only pretending to be ill again
exercise: Jayden laughed during the scary game. What could be true? | He might be fine or might be hiding fear | He must be a monster himself | Laughing means the game is safe for everyone | He can't see the screen properly
exercise: How did Priya's mum find out what was wrong? | She noticed the monster drawings and asked gently | She read Priya's diary | She shouted at Priya until she told her | The teacher sent a letter home about the drawings
exercise: Why did saying it out loud help Priya? | Talking about it makes it smaller | Saying words out loud scares the monsters away | Her mum magically fixed it | It didn't help at all
exercise: What was in Priya's bedtime plan? | No screens before bed and a funny audiobook | A scary film every night to get used to it | Sleeping in the garden | Staying up until she was too tired
exercise: Leo's little brother wants to see «the scary game». What should Leo do? | Say no, it's too scary for him | Show him just one bit | Show him when their parents are out | Describe all the gory parts instead
exercise: What is true about the monsters in games? | They are made by artists and actors | They live somewhere far away in the world | Some of them are real animals in disguise | They can come out of the screen at night
exercise: What kind of courage is saying «that game scared me»? | Being honest about a feeling | Fighting the monster in the game | Playing it again alone in the dark | Laughing louder than everyone
exercise: What could Leo have done at 3 a.m. instead of lying stiff for an hour? | Gone to his parents straight away | Looked under the bed with no light | Played the game again | Stayed silent until morning
exercise: Is it babyish to get nightmares from a scary game? | No, it's normal at any age | Yes, only babies and toddlers get them | Yes, after age 8 you can't get them | No, but only for girls, not boys
test: «lol leo slept in his mum's bed coz of a GAME». Fair to laugh? | No, getting scared by a scary game is normal | Yes, he's a baby if he's scared of a game | Yes, games can't scare anyone | Only if he's over 9
test: «i watch scary clips in bed every night, helps me sleep». Likely true? | No, they often cause bad dreams | Yes, a bit of fear makes you tired so you sleep better | Yes, it trains your brain | Only on a tablet
test: «ruby keeps seeing that one bit when the light's off, weird». Is it weird? | No, frightening pictures often come back in the dark | Yes, she must have special eyes that see in the dark | Yes, it means she's ill | No, she's making it up
test: «me? scared? never.» *checks behind door twice*. What's going on? | Zara is hiding that she's scared | Zara is looking for treasure under the bed | Zara is never scared | Zara has lost her phone
test: «ollie's got tummy ache AGAIN, every time we mention the game». Why might that be? | Worry about the game shows up in his tummy | He's allergic to something in Theo's house, like the dog | He hates the controller | He's faking to get sweets
test: Priya's mum sees monsters all over her homework. What's the best thing to do? | Ask gently where they came from | Tear the homework up | Shout at her for scribbling | Ignore it and say nothing at all
test: «leo's little bro wants to see the scary game, just show him, he's 6». Good idea? | No, it would scare him even more than Leo | Yes, he needs to toughen up like the big kids | Yes, if he sits at the back | Only the bloodiest bit
test: «it's only a skeleton made of pixels mate». Does knowing that help? | Yes, remembering it was made by artists can help | No, pixels really can climb out of screens at night | No, it makes it scarier | Yes, so watch it again tonight
test: «can't sleep after that game, what do i do». Wise advice? | Tell a grown-up and do something calm before bed | Watch more scary clips till you're used to it | Stay awake all night | Keep it secret so nobody laughs
test: «jayden laughed at the scary bit so he's the bravest». Does laughing prove bravery? | No, people laugh when they're nervous too | Yes, laughing always means you're not scared | Yes, he's the bravest in school | No, laughing means he's ill
test: Teacher: «Why do scary images stay with us so long?» | The brain stores danger strongly to protect us | Our eyes take photos of them like a camera | They are printed on our dreams | They don't, that's a myth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which frightening images are retained in memory with particular persistence. | The brain attends strongly to perceived danger, a disposition which assisted survival | The images are permanently recorded upon the retina | Only individuals of weak character retain such images | Screens emit signals which imprint images upon the brain
exam: Ascertain which practice is most likely to diminish the incidence of disturbing dreams following exposure to frightening material. | The avoidance of screens during the hour preceding sleep | The viewing of further frightening material at bedtime | The prolongation of wakefulness until exhaustion | The concealment of the fear from all adults
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an abdominal pain arising whenever a feared activity is proposed? | It may be a physical manifestation of anxiety | It is invariably caused by food eaten beforehand | It is necessarily feigned | It indicates an allergy to the activity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who, upon disclosure of a frightening image to a trusted adult, finds the fear diminished. | Discussing a fear with a trusted adult commonly reduces it | Disclosure invariably intensifies fear in the child | Fear can be reduced only by further exposure to the frightening images | Disclosure is of no effect whatsoever
